---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import RepresentativePublications from '../components/RepresentativePublications.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getPublicationByDoi } from '../data/publications';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const representativePublications = {
	inverseDesign: [
		'10.1103/bxw4-7y5c',
		'10.1016/j.matt.2025.102378',
		'10.1038/s41467-022-30818-2',
	].map(getPublicationByDoi),
	predictiveSynthesis: [
		'10.1002/aenm.202400163',
		'10.1038/s41467-023-42538-2',
		'10.1039/C5TC01535A',
	].map(getPublicationByDoi),
	defectsAndInterfaces: [
		'10.1021/acs.jpclett.2c03674',
		'10.1021/acs.jpclett.0c03663',
		'10.1021/acs.nanolett.8b04159',
	].map(getPublicationByDoi),
};
---

<Layout
	title="Research | Gupta Research Group"
	description="Research directions of the Gupta Research Group at the University of Central Florida."
>
	<Header />
	<main>
		<PageIntro label="Research" title="Research Directions">
			<p>
				We employ computational approaches to address fundamental challenges in two-dimensional materials for quantum and electronic applications, spanning inverse materials design, synthesis science, and defect and interface engineering. Our expertise is in density functional theory (DFT), molecular dynamics (MD), machine-learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs), transport modeling, and high-throughput computations.
			</p>
		</PageIntro>

		<div class="research-directions page-inner">
			<section class="research-feature" id="inverse-design" aria-labelledby="inverse-design-title">
				<div class="feature-layout">
				<figure class="feature-image">
					<img src={`${base}/images/main_inverse_design.png`} alt="Scientific visualization representing inverse design of low-dimensional quantum materials" width="3308" height="2475" />
				</figure>
				<div class="feature-copy">
					<p class="research-label">01 <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> Inverse Design</p>
					<h2 id="inverse-design-title">Inverse Design of 2D Quantum Materials</h2>
					<p>Discovering materials with targeted functionalities is central to next-generation electronic and quantum technologies. But must new materials always be discovered to realize new functionalities? We aim to establish a new paradigm in which topographical deformations are used to engineer emergent electronic and quantum phases in 2D materials, moving beyond serendipitous materials discovery toward geometry-driven design.</p>
					<details class="research-details">
						<summary><span class="details-more">More about this research ↓</span><span class="details-less">Less about this research ↑</span></summary>
						<p>Using multiscale computational approaches, we investigate how deformation-induced pseudo-electric and pseudo-magnetic fields reshape electronic structure and give rise to correlated electronic states and exotic optical and magnetic properties. By connecting a prescribed deformation profile directly to an effective electronic Hamiltonian, we seek to develop general design rules for creating targeted quantum phases through geometry. This approach could provide a versatile route to tune spin, charge, optical, and magnetic behavior without changing the underlying material composition. Ultimately, this framework could enable tunable quantum functionality for sensing, logic, memory, and other emerging electronic technologies.</p>
					</details>
				</div>
				</div>
				<RepresentativePublications publications={representativePublications.inverseDesign} />
			</section>

			<section class="research-feature feature-reversed" id="synthesis-science" aria-labelledby="synthesis-science-title">
				<div class="feature-layout">
				<div class="feature-copy">
					<p class="research-label">02 <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> Synthesis Science</p>
					<h2 id="synthesis-science-title">Predictive Synthesis</h2>
					<p>Synthesis remains a major bottleneck in realizing the potential of 2D materials. Can modeling approaches predict synthesis conditions rather than merely explain growth <em>post facto</em>? Using ML-based interatomic potentials coupled with thermodynamic and kinetic models, we develop predictive frameworks for CVD growth that connect precursor chemistry, nucleation, and crystal growth across multiple time and length scales.</p>
					<details class="research-details">
						<summary><span class="details-more">More about this research ↓</span><span class="details-less">Less about this research ↑</span></summary>
						<p>We are also broadly interested in extending these approaches to predict synthesis pathways and processing conditions for bulk functional materials. Ultimately, this work aims to establish a general framework for rational synthesis design, enabling lower growth temperatures, reduced defect densities, improved crystal quality, and more reliable synthesis of targeted materials and phases.</p>
					</details>
				</div>
				<figure class="feature-image synthesis-image">
					<img src={`${base}/images/main_synthesis_science.png`} alt="Scientific visualization of molecular deposition and material formation on a surface" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" />
				</figure>
				</div>
				<RepresentativePublications publications={representativePublications.predictiveSynthesis} />
			</section>

			<section class="research-feature" id="defects-interfaces" aria-labelledby="defects-interfaces-title">
				<div class="feature-layout">
				<figure class="feature-image">
					<img src={`${base}/images/main_defect_interface.png`} alt="Atomic-scale scientific visualization of defects and interfaces in a material" width="1450" height="1085" loading="lazy" />
				</figure>
				<div class="feature-copy">
					<p class="research-label">03 <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> Defects &amp; Interfaces</p>
					<h2 id="defects-interfaces-title">Defect &amp; Interface Design</h2>
					<p>Although defects and interfaces are imperfections in materials, they are critical for integrating materials into functional devices. Can we develop a predictive framework for designing them during integration of 2D materials into quantum and electronic technologies? We aim to develop computational design principles for defects, interfaces, and quantum coherence.</p>
					<details class="research-details">
						<summary><span class="details-more">More about this research ↓</span><span class="details-less">Less about this research ↑</span></summary>
						<p>For quantum technologies, we use high-throughput first-principles and open-quantum-system approaches to identify defect-based spin-photonic qubits with favorable optical, spin, and coherence properties and to understand their decoherence mechanisms. For advanced microelectronics, we combine first-principles calculations, continuum electrostatics, and quantum transport simulations to investigate contacts, doping, interfaces, and transport in emerging 2D materials. Ultimately, this work will enable rational design of materials and device architectures for quantum information processing and energy-efficient electronics.</p>
					</details>
				</div>
				</div>
				<RepresentativePublications publications={representativePublications.defectsAndInterfaces} />
			</section>
		</div>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
	main { overflow: hidden; background: #fff; }
	.page-inner { width: min(100% - 64px, 1280px); margin-inline: auto; }
	.section-label { margin: 0; color: #896400; font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 750; letter-spacing: 0.15em; text-transform: uppercase; }
	h2 { font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -0.04em; }
	.research-directions { padding-bottom: clamp(56px, 6vw, 88px); }
	.research-feature { position: relative; padding-block: clamp(72px, 6.5vw, 92px); scroll-margin-top: 24px; }
	.research-feature:first-child { padding-top: clamp(56px, 5vw, 68px); }
	.research-feature:nth-child(even) { background: #fcfbf9; box-shadow: 0 0 0 100vmax #fcfbf9; clip-path: inset(0 -100vmax); }
	.research-feature + .research-feature { border-top: 1px solid #e5e5e2; }
	.feature-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 47fr) minmax(0, 53fr); gap: clamp(48px, 6vw, 88px); align-items: start; }
	.feature-reversed .feature-layout { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 53fr) minmax(0, 47fr); }
	.feature-image { margin: 0; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; overflow: hidden; background: #f7f8f8; }
	.feature-image img { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: contain; object-position: center; }
	.synthesis-image img { object-fit: cover; object-position: center; transform: scale(1.015); }
	.feature-copy { max-width: 630px; }
	.research-label { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #896400; font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 750; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; }
	.research-label span { color: #c99700; }
	.feature-copy h2 { margin: 0; font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 3.5vw, 3.75rem); line-height: 1.08; text-wrap: balance; }
	.feature-copy p { max-width: 590px; margin: 24px 0 0; color: #555a63; font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.2vw, 1.12rem); line-height: 1.72; }
	.research-details { margin-top: 20px; }
	.research-details summary { width: fit-content; padding: 4px 0 7px; border-bottom: 1px solid #c99700; color: #292c32; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.84rem; font-weight: 620; list-style: none; transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease; }
	.research-details summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
	.research-details summary:hover { color: #6f5200; border-color: #896400; }
	.research-details summary:focus-visible { color: #6f5200; outline: 2px solid #c99700; outline-offset: 4px; }
	.research-details .details-less { display: none; }
	.research-details[open] .details-more { display: none; }
	.research-details[open] .details-less { display: inline; }
	.feature-copy .research-details p { margin-top: 20px; }

	@media (min-width: 801px) {
		#inverse-design .feature-copy { align-self: start; }
		#inverse-design .research-label { margin-bottom: 12px; }
		#inverse-design .feature-copy > p:not(.research-label) { margin-top: 16px; }
		:is(#inverse-design, #synthesis-science, #defects-interfaces) .feature-copy > p:not(.research-label),
		:is(#inverse-design, #synthesis-science, #defects-interfaces) .research-details p { color: #555a63; font-family: inherit; font-size: clamp(0.97rem, 1.18vw, 1.08rem); font-weight: inherit; letter-spacing: inherit; line-height: 1.6; }
		#inverse-design .research-details { margin-top: 12px; }
	}
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.research-details summary { transition: none; }
	}

	@media (max-width: 800px) {
		.feature-layout, .feature-reversed .feature-layout { gap: 40px; }
		.feature-copy h2 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem); }
	}

	@media (max-width: 600px) {
		.page-inner { width: min(100% - 40px, 1280px); }
		.research-directions { padding-bottom: 28px; }
		.research-feature { padding-block: 50px; }
		.feature-layout, .feature-reversed .feature-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 30px; }
		.research-feature:first-child { padding-top: 48px; }
		.research-feature .feature-image { order: -1; }
		.synthesis-image img { object-fit: contain; transform: none; }
		.feature-copy h2 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 9vw, 2.65rem); line-height: 1.12; }
		.feature-copy p { margin-top: 20px; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.68; }
	}
</style>
